WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:09.270
MARKUS KLUTE: Hello.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:13.480
So in the last video, we
looked at ranges of forces.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.079
So we already saw one
aspect, and wanted

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.510
to learn about what
kind of interaction

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.170
happens when you study
a specific force.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.610
You learn about
the force carrier

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:28.140
from noting the range in
which forces interact.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.540
In this class, we
talk about decays.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.880
But in more general
terms, when we

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.690
want to measure
properties of forces,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:37.150
we have basically
three concepts at hand

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.150
which can be
experimentally determined.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.980
The first one is
masses of bound states.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.190
| you might remember
from atomic physics

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.220
that you learn a lot about
electromagnetic interaction

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.180
by studying, for example, the
hydrogen atom, where you have

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.790
an electron circling
around the program,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.060
and you can study
in detail aspects

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.690
of the electromagnetic
interaction.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.870
The second aspect is decay
rates of unstable particles,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.280
or the width of an
unstable particle.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.880
So in quantum mechanics,
the lifetime of the particle

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.560
is related to the width.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.660
And so that's what we're going
to discuss in this video.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.260
And then lastly, we can look
at the reaction rates expressed

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.310
as cross sections.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.810
So that's the topic of
the next video after this.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.590
Let's talk about decays.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.870
So we can define this new
symbol, the decay rate lambda,

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.050
and s as a function
of time, probability

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.700
that a particle will survive
at least until some time t.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.540
We can now discuss this,
and say, the probability

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.460
at some time t relates to
the probability at some time

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:50.990
t plus delta t by the likelihood
1 minus the decay rate

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.620
times the time
interval, delta t.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.630
And so from this, we find that
the change of the probability

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.810
for the particle to survive
is proportional to that

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.250
probability and decay rate.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.580
So now if we integrate
this, we find

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.410
that the log of this probability
is equal to some constant times

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.540
lambda decay rate times time.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.850
So now, if you simply assume
that the particle existed

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:24.190
at the initial time t, we
said we set k equal to 0.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.130
We find this very
famous exponential decay

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:31.080
law, e to the power
of minus lambda t.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.650
And this is shown here in this
picture as this exponential.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.320
So far, so good.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.530
We can now define and look at
this distribution a little bit

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.610
more.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.590
We can, for example,
look at the average time

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:49.450
for that a particle lives,
because the average time

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.520
tau is simply given by the
integral from 0 to infinity.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.630
So we basically integrate
over this distribution

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.390
to get the average
time for the particle.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.560
And that's equal to 1 over
the decay rate, 1 over lambda.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.590
You can do the algebra,
yourself, in fact,

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.480
to follow this.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.310
So if you now express this
probability for the particle

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.850
to survive until some time
t through the lifetime,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.570
you find this is equal
to e to the minus t

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.590
over the lifetime
of the particle.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.920
So you might not want
to look at one particle,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.840
but a sum of particles
and look, at the time

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.350
dependence of the number of
particles which survived.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.363
Because it's equal to
the number of particles

00:03:41.363 --> 00:03:43.530
as a function of time is
equal to the initial number

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.330
of particles times
the probability

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.360
that any given
particle survives.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.210
And that's, again, given
by this exponential.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.020
In nuclear physics,
one often talks

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.220
about the half life, half
life time of the particle

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.860
or of [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.930
And that's given,
as you would assume,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.980
by the time it takes for half
of the particles to decay.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:17.179
So N of tau 1/2 is equal to the
number of initial particles, N0

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:18.410
over 2.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:23.000
And you find, then,
that this half life

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.400
is related to the
lifetime of the particle

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.115
with a factor of about 2/3.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.870
This leads to some confusion
in numerical values

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:39.790
sometimes when you ask for
specific answers from up here,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.720
in experiments.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.960
All right, so there's another
aspect of decays which arises,

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.920
which comes from a fundamental
property of quantum mechanics.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.560
So if you have an
unstable state,

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.310
or any unstable state does
not have exact energy state,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:02.710
it just follows, if you want,
from the uncertainty principle.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:09.320
So the width of the
particle is quantized,

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.170
and it's quantized
with this lambda here.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.940
And if you can see,
lambda relates, again,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:18.740
to the decay rate or the
lifetime of the particle.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.650
Another complication
can occur when

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.830
there's multiple ways for
the particle to decay.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.140
For example, you
have a Higgs boson

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:28.730
as we see on the
next slide, which

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.440
might decay into multiple--

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.230
has a way to decay into
multiple particles.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:38.140
Here, we define a partial
width, where the partial width

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:42.260
is defined as half the
width of the particle

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.468
to decay into a specific mode.

00:05:44.468 --> 00:05:46.135
And then the total
width of the particle

00:05:46.135 --> 00:05:48.340
is given by the sum
of the partial widths

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.850
of all possible ways for
the particle to decay.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.880
Using this, you
can also calculate

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.690
the likelihood of a particle
to decay in a specific way.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.640
That's called the
"branching fraction."

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.310
And that's given by the
partial width divided

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.650
by the total width, or the
partial decay weight divided

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:08.940
by the total decay rate.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.300
Again, the total has to
be 1, the probability

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.450
for a particle to
decay in any mode is 1.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:19.240
Therefore, the sum of the
branching ratios is 1 as well.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.720
All right, so looking
at a specific example,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:25.990
the Higgs boson is probably
my favorite example

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.180
in this entire class.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.750
You find here, given
branching fractions or ratios

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.600
of specific decay modes,
because it's not always in which

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.250
the Higgs boson decay,
but the most dominant one.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.270
The most prominent one is the
one with Higgs boson decays

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.030
into a pair of b quarks.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.030
We will later see, maybe
even as an exercise,

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:49.150
why the distribution
function of branching ratios

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.005
is the way it's
being shown here.

00:06:51.005 --> 00:06:52.880
The Higgs boson has been
measured with a mass

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.320
of 125 gV and a little bit.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.320
And you see at this mass
here, the prominent decay mode

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.120
is simply the b
bar, but it's also

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.270
possible for the exponential to
decay into a pair of W bosons,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:10.450
where an interesting
loop diagram into gluons.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:14.500
Even so, gluons are massless,
or tau, charm, Z bosons, and so

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.710
on.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:19.540
And we just showed you in a
paper which was submitted today

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.000
to the arXiv, the
Higgs boson also

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.380
can decay into a pair of muons
with a branching ratio of 2

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.820
times 10 to the minus 4.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.840
So it's rather rare,
but it's possible.

